<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<section xml:id="section-differentiable-discover" >
<title>Discovering Differentiability</title>

<introduction>
    <p>Standards:
        <ul>
            <li>Identify portions of a curve that are differentiable </li>
        </ul>
    </p>

</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-derivative-language">
    <title>Terminology</title>

    <p>The grammar of derivatives is not uniform. The noun is <term>derivative</term>, the verb is <term>differentiate</term>, and the adjective is <term>differentiable</term>.</p>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-differentiable-discover">
    <title>Differentiable Appearance</title>

    <definition xml:id="definition-differentiable">
        <title>Differentiable</title>
        <statement><p>A function <m>f(x)</m> is <term>differentiable</term> at <m>x=a</m> if and only if the derivative of <m>f(x)</m> exists at <m>x=a.</m></p></statement>
    </definition>

    <p>If a curve is differentiable on all points in an interval we say the curve is differentiable on that interval.</p>

    <p>This definition is what we expect, but it does not tell us what a differentiable curve looks like. To determine this we will use the interpretation of the derivative as the slope of the tangent line and discover what a function looks like where it is differentiable and what it looks like where it is not differentiable. </p>

    <p>The definition of derivative is a two sided limit. This means the curve will be not differentiable when the left and right sides of that limit do not match. There will be a jump in the slopes.</p>

    <activity xml:id="activity-differentiable">
        <title>Differentiable Points</title>
        <introduction><p>The goal of this activity is to visualize a derivative existing by comparing left and right secants (line connecting two points on a curve).</p></introduction>
        <task><p>Using <xref ref="interactive-differentiable-yes" /> move point A from <m>x=-1</m> toward point B. Note the change of the slope m (shown on the left). To what does it appear to be approaching?</p></task>
        <task><p>Using <xref ref="interactive-differentiable-yes" /> move point A from <m>x=1</m> toward point B. Note the change of the slope m (shown on the left). To what does it appear to be approaching?</p></task>
        <task><p>Do the two approximations of the tangent slope above match? If so, what does this imply about the (2-sided) limit?</p></task>
    </activity>

    <figure xml:id="interactive-differentiable-yes">
        <caption>Illustration of Differentiability</caption>
        <interactive platform="geogebra" width="100%" aspect="4:3" preview="images/Differentiable.png" label="interactive-differentiable-yes">
            <slate xml:id="geogebra-differentiable-yes" surface="geogebra" material="mzzskfua" aspect="4:3">
                    setCoordSystem(-1, 6, -1, 6);
                    enableShiftDragZoom(true);
            </slate>
            <instructions>
                <p>Move point A and track the slope m. Check that the secant slopes on the left approach the same value as the secant slopes on the right.</p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <activity xml:id="activity-not-differentiable">
        <title>Non-Differentiable Points</title>
        <introduction><p>The goal of this activity is to determine what the graph of a function looks like where it is not differentiable.</p></introduction>
        <task><p>Using <xref ref="interactive-differentiable-no" /> move point A from <m>x=-1</m> toward point B. Note the change of the slope m (shown on the left). To what does it appear to be approaching?</p></task>
        <task><p>Using <xref ref="interactive-differentiable-no" /> move point A from <m>x=1</m> toward point B. Note the change of the slope m (shown on the left). To what does it appear to be approaching?</p></task>
        <task><p>Do the two approximations of the tangent slope above match? If not, what kind of jump is it?</p></task>
    </activity>

    <figure xml:id="interactive-differentiable-no">
        <caption>Illustration of Non-Differentiability</caption>
        <interactive platform="geogebra" width="100%" aspect="4:3" preview="images/NonDifferentiable.png" label="interactive-differentiable-no">
            <slate xml:id="geogebra-differentiable-no" surface="geogebra" material="zj6hjv7z" aspect="4:3">
                    setCoordSystem(-1, 6, -1, 6);
                    enableShiftDragZoom(true);
            </slate>
            <instructions>
                <p>Move point A and track the slope m. Compare the secant slopes on the left to the secant slopes on the right.</p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <activity xml:id="activity-differentiable-zoom">
        <title>Appearance of Differentiable Curves</title>
        <introduction><p>The goal of this activity is to determine the difference in appearance between an interval on a curve that is differentiable versus one that includes a non-differentiable point.</p></introduction>
        <task><p>Using <xref ref="interactive-differentiable-yes" /> zoom in around point B until the graph becomes very boring. What does the curve look like when zoomed in this far?</p></task>
        <task><p>Using <xref ref="interactive-differentiable-no" /> zoom in around point B the same amount as in the previous task. How does the appearance of the curve near point B change on this curve?</p></task>
        <task><p>To see this property we have to zoom in far enough. This means the trait is true on some interval (possibly small). Where have we used this concept before in calculus?</p></task>
    </activity>

</subsection>

</section>
